- How does the approach change if teleports have costs or limited usage per row/col?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to model non-crossing, higher-arity structures with DP and reconstruct a solution.

## Key Concepts

- DP state dp[i][t] for i and t
- Non-crossing triple formation
- Reconstruction via backpointers
- Time/space trade-offs and optimization path

## Code Example

```javascript
function maxTriples(A, n, k) {
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(-Infinity));
  const prv = Array.from({length: n+1}, () => Array(k+1).fill(null));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for (let i=1; i<=n; i++) {
    for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
      dp[i][t] = dp[i-1][t];
      if (t>0) {
        for (let i1=1; i1<i; i1++) {
          for (let j=i1+1; j<i; j++) {
            const prev = (i1>=2 ? dp[i1-1][t-1] : (t-1===0 ? 0 : -Infinity));
            const val = prev + A[i1-1]*A[j-1]*A[i-1];
            if (val > dp[i][t]) { dp[i][t] = val; prv[i][t] = {i1, j}; }
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction logic would backtrack using prv
  return {value: dp[n][k], triples: []};
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP over prefixes with a non-linear subarray cost (max) and requires reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP over prefixes and partition count
- On-the-fly max for subarray cost
- Reconstruction via prev pointers
- Time/space trade-offs (O(n^2 k) time, O(nk) space)

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartition(A, k){
  const n = A.length;
  const INF = 1e15;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(INF));
  const prev = Array.from({length: n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
  dp[0][0] = 0;
  for(let i=1; i<=n; i++){
    for(let t=1; t<=Math.min(i,k); t++){
      let curMax = -Infinity;
      for(let p=i-1; p>=t-1; p--){
        curMax = Math.max(curMax, A[p]);
        const val = dp[p][t-1] + curMax;
        if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; prev[i][t] = p; }
      }
    }
  }
  return {cost: dp[n][k], prev};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize to O(nk) if cost allowed a Monge property? 
- How would you extend to allow a penalty for creating a new subarray?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a DP with a non-trivial cost function and prefix optimization.

## Key Concepts
- DP on prefixes and partitions
- O(1) substring cost via precomputed diff/pref
- Path reconstruction via parent pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
// Precompute diff and prefix
let diff = Array(n+1).fill(0);
for (let i=2;i<=n;i++) diff[i] = s[i-1] !== s[i-2] ? 1:0;
let pref = Array(n+1).fill(0);
for (let i=2;i<=n;i++) pref[i] = pref[i-1] + diff[i];

// cost(a,b)
function cost(a,b){ return pref[b] - pref[a]; }

// DP
let dp = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(Infinity));
let par = Array.from({length:n+1}, ()=> Array(k+1).fill(-1));
for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){ dp[i][1] = cost(1,i); }

for(let t=2;t<=k;t++){
  for(let i=t;i<=n;i++){
    for(let p=t-1;p<=i-1;p++){
      let val = dp[p][t-1] + cost(p+1,i);
      if(val < dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t] = val; par[i][t] = p; }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How could you optimize to O(nk) with divide-and-conquer DP if cost satisfies quadrangle inequality?
- How would you extract the actual partition end indices from par?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests DP design with an extra edit dimension on a classic string problem; requires careful transitions for matches, edits, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- DP on substrings (i, j)
- Extra dimension for edits (t)
- Transitions for match/mismatch with edits
- Path reconstruction and complexity trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxLPSWithEdits(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () => Array.from({length: n}, () => Array(k+1).fill(0)));
  for (let i=0;i<n;i++) for (let t=0;t<=k;t++) dp[i][i][t] = 1;
  for (let len=2; len<=n; len++) {
    for (let i=0; i+len-1<n; i++) {
      const j = i+len-1;
      for (let t=0; t<=k; t++) {
        let best = Math.max(dp[i+1][j][t], dp[i][j-1][t]);
        if (s[i] === s[j]) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t] + 2);
        if (t >= 1) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-1] + 2);
        if (t >= 2) best = Math.max(best, dp[i+1][j-1][t-2] + 2);
        dp[i][j][t] = best;
      }
    }
  }
  return dp[0][n-1][k];
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How to require exactly k edits instead of at most?
- How to reduce space to O(n*k)?
- How to reconstruct the edited string and the subsequence?

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests a foundational dynamic programming pattern—selecting k non-adjacent elements with maximum sum. It assesses understanding of state definition, transition logic, and reconstruction, making it a classic beginner DP problem.

## Key Concepts
- **State definition**: dp[i][j] represents the optimal sum for the first i elements with exactly j selections
- **Transition logic**: Compare skipping the current element vs. taking it (with the appropriate previous state)
- **Backtracking**: Reconstruct chosen indices by comparing states during backtracking
- **Feasibility constraint**: Maximum selections possible in i elements is floor((i+1)/2)

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxSumNonAdjacent(A, m) {
  const n = A.length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(m + 1).fill(NEG));

  for (let i = 0; i <= n; i++) dp[i][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let j = 1; j <= Math.min(m, Math.floor((i + 1) / 2)); j++) {
      const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
      const skip = dp[i - 1][j];
      dp[i][j] = Math.max(skip, take);
    }
  }

  const result = dp[n][m];
  const indices = [];
  let i = n, j = m;

  while (i > 0 && j > 0) {
    const take = A[i - 1] + (i >= 2 ? dp[i - 2][j - 1] : (j - 1 === 0 ? 0 : NEG));
    if (dp[i][j] === take) {
      indices.push(i - 1);
      j--;
      i -= 2;
    } else {
      i--;
    }
  }

  return { sum: result, indices: indices.reverse() };
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(n × m)
**Space Complexity:** O(n × m), or O(m) with optimization.

## Why This Is Asked
This problem tests understanding of partition dynamic programming with nontrivial segment costs, requiring careful recurrence formulation, efficient range query handling, and solution reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Partition DP with fixed number of contiguous segments
- Segment cost computation using range max-min queries
- Reconstruction via predecessor pointers
- Time/space optimization techniques

## Code Example
```javascript
function minCostPartitions(A, k) {
  const n = A.length;
  const dp = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const pre = Array.from({ length: n + 1 }, () => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  // Base case: single segment
  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    let maxVal = A[0], minVal = A[0];
    for (let j = 0; j < i; j++) {
      maxVal = Math.max(maxVal, A[j]);
      minVal = Math.min(minVal, A[j]);
      dp[i][1] = maxVal - minVal;
    }
  }

  // DP transitions
  for (let t = 2; t <= k; t++) {
    for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const segmentCost = computeCost(A, j + 1, i);
        if (dp[j][t - 1] + segmentCost < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[j][t - 1] + segmentCost;
          pre[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct segments
  const segments = [];
  let end = n;
  for (let t = k; t >= 1; t--) {
    const start = pre[end][t] + 1;
    segments.unshift([start, end]);
    end = pre[end][t];
  }

  return { minCost: dp[n][k], segments };
}
```

## Why This Question Is Asked
This problem assesses your ability to handle non-trivial path constraints in dynamic programming, specifically exact turn counting. It also requires path reconstruction, reflecting real-world interview complexity where identifying a solution isn't sufficient—you must also provide the actual path.

## Key Concepts
- **4D DP state** capturing both position and path history: `(i, j, t, dir)` tracks where you are, how many turns you've made, and your incoming direction
- **Turn counting** via conditional transitions: staying in the same direction doesn't increment the turn count, while switching does
- **Backpointer-based reconstruction** stores the predecessor for each state, enabling full path recovery

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxPathWithKTurns(grid, k) {
  const n = grid.length, m = grid[0].length;
  const dp = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k + 1}, () => [-Infinity, -Infinity])
    )
  );
  dp[0][0][0][0] = dp[0][0][0][1] = grid[0][0];

  const parent = Array.from({length: n}, () =>
    Array.from({length: m}, () =>
      Array.from({length: k + 1}, () => [null, null])
    )
  );

  for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    for (let j = 0; j < m; j++) {
      for (let t = 0; t <= k; t++) {
        if (i === 0 && j === 0) continue;
        if (j > 0) {
          const fromRight = dp[i][j-1][t][0];
          const fromDown = t > 0 ? dp[i][j-1][t-1][1] : -Infinity;
          if (fromRight >= fromDown) {
            dp[i][j][t][0] = fromRight + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][0] = [i, j-1, t, 0];
          } else {
            dp[i][j][t][0] = fromDown + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][0] = [i, j-1, t-1, 1];
          }
        }
        if (i > 0) {
          const fromDown = dp[i-1][j][t][1];
          const fromRight = t > 0 ? dp[i-1][j][t-1][0] : -Infinity;
          if (fromDown >= fromRight) {
            dp[i][j][t][1] = fromDown + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][1] = [i-1, j, t, 1];
          } else {
            dp[i][j][t][1] = fromRight + grid[i][j];
            parent[i][j][t][1] = [i-1, j, t-1, 0];
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }

  const endDir = dp[n-1][m-1][k][0] >= dp[n-1][m-1][k][1] ? 0 : 1;
  const maxSum = dp[n-1][m-1][k][endDir];
  const path = [];
  let cur = [n-1, m-1, k, endDir];

  while (cur) {
    path.unshift([cur[0], cur[1]]);
    cur = parent[cur[0]][cur[1]][cur[2]][cur[3]];
  }

  return { sum: maxSum, path };
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Introduces a grid-path DP with a bounded number of teleports, forcing state compression and a 2D prefix max to keep complexity at O(KRC). It tests correctness, backtracking, and memory considerations beyond standard path DP.

## Key Concepts
- DP on grid with at most k teleports
- 2D prefix max to propagate bestPrev for teleport transitions
- Reconstruction: store choice or re-run DP with pointers

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxEnergy(grid, k) {
  const R = grid.length, C = grid[0].length;
  const NEG = -Infinity;
  let dpPrev = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
  dpPrev[0][0] = grid[0][0];
  for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
    const bestPrev = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
    for (let r = 0; r < R; r++) {
      for (let c = 0; c < C; c++) {
        bestPrev[r][c] = dpPrev[r][c];
        if (r > 0) bestPrev[r][c] = Math.max(bestPrev[r][c], bestPrev[r-1][c]);
        if (c > 0) bestPrev[r][c] = Math.max(bestPrev[r][c], bestPrev[r][c-1]);
      }
    }
    const dpCurr = Array.from({length:R}, () => Array(C).fill(NEG));
    for (let r = 0; r < R; r++) {
      for (let c = 0; c < C; c++) {
        if (r === 0 && c === 0) continue;
        let best = NEG;
        if (r > 0) best = Math.max(best, dpCurr[r-1][c]);
        if (c > 0) best = Math.max(best, dpCurr[r][c-1]);
        if (bestPrev[r][c] !== NEG) best = Math.max(best, bestPrev[r][c]);
        if (best !== NEG) dpCurr[r][c] = best + grid[r][c];
      }
    }
    dpPrev = dpCurr;
  }
  return dpPrev[R-1][C-1];
}
```

